# ============================================================
# LUMINA MATH — UI/UX ENGINEERING RULES
# ============================================================

You are working on an existing React + TypeScript + Vite educational
mathematics application.

The application is called Lumina / SIH Demo.

Your job is to significantly improve the visual quality, interaction design,
animation, responsiveness and perceived polish of the existing application.

DO NOT rebuild the application from scratch.

DO NOT replace the existing content architecture.

DO NOT rewrite the mathematical content.

DO NOT remove existing learning functionality.

The existing content/data is the source of truth.

============================================================
1. EXISTING ARCHITECTURE
============================================================

The project contains:

client/
    React + TypeScript + Vite frontend

server/
    Hono + Cloudflare Workers backend

The frontend contains:
- React Router
- topic/roadmap pages
- learning modules
- concept pages
- interactive visualizations
- exploration/application pages
- tutor/AI functionality
- JSON-driven educational content

Preserve this architecture.

Before modifying any component:
1. Inspect the existing component.
2. Understand its props and state.
3. Understand where it is rendered.
4. Preserve its existing functionality.
5. Modify presentation before modifying behavior.

============================================================
2. PRIMARY UI OBJECTIVE
============================================================

Transform the current UI into a premium modern educational platform.

Design characteristics:

- light
- warm
- sophisticated
- editorial
- curious
- playful but not childish
- mathematical
- spacious
- highly interactive
- visually memorable

DO NOT use dark full-page backgrounds.

DO NOT make black the dominant page background.

Dark colors may be used for:
- text
- small labels
- buttons
- isolated visual elements
- contrast sections

but the overall interface must remain LIGHT.

Preferred backgrounds:

#F7F8F5
#FAFAF7
#F3F4F0
#FFFFFF

Preferred text:

#171A24
#272A32
#555963
#777B84

Preferred accents:

Coral:
#FF695D

Warm yellow:
#F4C95D

Soft blue:
#7CA7D9

Soft green:
#87B89A

Lavender:
#A79BD8

Use accents selectively.

Avoid excessive saturated colors.

============================================================
3. DESIGN LANGUAGE
============================================================

The visual language should feel like:

"Apple-level simplicity + editorial magazine + interactive
mathematics laboratory."

Use:

- oversized typography
- generous whitespace
- asymmetric layouts
- large numbers
- subtle borders
- rounded cards
- editorial labels
- visual hierarchy
- layered compositions
- restrained shadows
- animated diagrams
- subtle texture when appropriate

Avoid:

- generic SaaS dashboards
- excessive cards
- excessive gradients
- neon colors
- dark dashboards
- glassmorphism everywhere
- excessive rounded pills
- excessive shadows
- template-like layouts
- unnecessary icons

============================================================
4. TYPOGRAPHY
============================================================

Use a strong hierarchy.

Hero headings:
- very large
- tight tracking
- short line lengths
- editorial feel

Example:

"Learn the mathematics
behind the world."

Use:
font-weight: 600–700
letter-spacing: -0.05em to -0.07em

Body:
comfortable line height
approximately 1.6

Labels:
small uppercase
letter spacing around .12em–.2em

Use serif/italic typography selectively for emphasis.

Example:

"One connected
story."

with "story" using a tasteful serif italic.

Do not use decorative typography everywhere.

============================================================
5. PAGE STRUCTURE
============================================================

Every major page should have:

1. clear entry point
2. strong hero
3. visual hook
4. content hierarchy
5. interactive elements
6. clear next action

Avoid immediately presenting a wall of cards.

Use visual rhythm:

hero
↓
breathing space
↓
visual
↓
content
↓
interaction
↓
content
↓
next step

============================================================
6. GSAP IS THE PRIMARY ANIMATION SYSTEM
============================================================

Use GSAP for substantial UI animation.

Install/use:

gsap

and:

ScrollTrigger

Import:

import gsap from "gsap";
import { ScrollTrigger } from "gsap/ScrollTrigger";

Register once:

gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);

Do not create animation systems with:
- setInterval
- arbitrary requestAnimationFrame loops
- excessive CSS keyframes
- multiple animation libraries for the same task

CSS transitions are fine for tiny hover interactions.

GSAP should control:
- page entrances
- scroll reveals
- staggered content
- SVG drawing
- large UI transitions
- counters
- parallax
- timeline-based interactions

============================================================
7. GSAP REACT RULES
============================================================

Use @gsap/react when appropriate.

Prefer:

useGSAP(() => {
    ...
}, { scope: container });

Avoid creating animations directly during render.

Always clean up ScrollTriggers and animations.

Never create duplicate ScrollTriggers after route changes.

Animations must survive React re-renders safely.

============================================================
8. ANIMATION PHILOSOPHY
============================================================

Animation must communicate something.

Good:

A triangle draws itself before explaining trigonometric ratios.

Good:

A roadmap node expands when selected.

Good:

A graph gradually appears as the user scrolls.

Good:

A number counts from 0 to its final value.

Bad:

Random floating shapes everywhere.

Bad:

Every card bouncing.

Bad:

Continuous animations that distract from reading.

Motion should answer:

"What changed?"

"What should I look at?"

"What should I interact with?"

============================================================
9. PAGE ENTRANCE ANIMATION
============================================================

Create a consistent page entrance.

Recommended sequence:

background
↓
eyebrow
↓
heading
↓
description
↓
CTA
↓
hero visualization

Use approximately:

duration: 0.5–0.9 seconds
ease: power3.out

Stagger:

0.06–0.12 seconds

Avoid slow theatrical animations.

Educational pages should feel responsive.

============================================================
10. SCROLLTRIGGER
============================================================

Use ScrollTrigger for:

- section reveals
- mathematical diagrams
- roadmap progression
- visualization activation
- horizontal galleries
- number counters
- SVG path drawing

Recommended:

gsap.from(element, {
    y: 40,
    opacity: 0,
    duration: 0.7,
    ease: "power3.out",
    scrollTrigger: {
        trigger: element,
        start: "top 82%",
        once: true
    }
});

Prefer once:true for ordinary content.

Do not create hundreds of independent ScrollTriggers.

============================================================
11. REDUCED MOTION
============================================================

Accessibility is mandatory.

Respect:

prefers-reduced-motion

If reduced motion is enabled:

- disable large transitions
- remove parallax
- remove continuous animations
- keep content visible immediately
- retain functional interactions

Never hide important content behind animation.

============================================================
12. BARBA.JS
============================================================

Barba.js may be used for page transitions.

IMPORTANT:

This is a React Router SPA.

DO NOT replace React Router with Barba.js.

DO NOT allow Barba.js to control React application routing.

Barba should be used only as a visual transition layer where compatible.

If implementing Barba:

- preserve React Router
- avoid duplicate route state
- avoid manipulating React-owned DOM after unmount
- destroy Barba hooks when the React root is destroyed
- do not create memory leaks
- do not hijack API calls
- do not break browser back/forward navigation

Preferred transition:

current page
↓
soft cream overlay
↓
new page content
↓
overlay reveals

Keep transition approximately 350–650ms.

Never use a black full-screen transition.

Use a light neutral / cream transition layer.

============================================================
13. ROUTING
============================================================

Existing React Router routes are authoritative.

Do not rename routes unless explicitly instructed.

Important routes include:

/subjects
/topics/trigonometry
/explore/applications/trigonometry
/roadmap/subject/:subjectId
/tutor/:conceptId

Do not break direct navigation to these routes.

React Router must continue working with Cloudflare Workers SPA fallback.

============================================================
14. TRIGONOMETRY EXPERIENCE
============================================================

Trigonometry is a flagship experience.

It has two distinct experiences:

A. Study Trigonometry

This contains modules:

1. Introduction to Trigonometry
2. Trigonometric Ratios & Functions
3. Trigonometric Equations

Applications of Trigonometry is NOT a curriculum module.

B. Explore Applications

This is the curiosity/visual exploration experience.

Do not merge these two systems.

============================================================
15. APPLICATIONS PAGE
============================================================

The Applications page should feel like an interactive laboratory.

Use:

- large visual cards
- animated diagrams
- interactive controls
- real-world scenarios
- curiosity questions
- progressive reveals

Applications should include concepts such as:

- tower height
- shadow measurement
- triangulation
- navigation
- waves
- unit circle
- sine wave
- similar triangles
- equation intersections

Do not turn these into ordinary text cards.

The visualization is the primary object.

============================================================
16. INTRODUCTION TO TRIGONOMETRY
============================================================

This section should maximize curiosity.

Prefer real-world scenarios:

- measuring buildings
- shadows
- camera angles
- rotating wheels
- clocks
- navigation
- surveying
- ramps
- satellite positioning
- circular motion

Whenever appropriate:

let the student manipulate:

angle
distance
height
radius
speed
time
direction

Then show mathematical consequences.

The student should discover relationships before being given formulas.

============================================================
17. VISUALIZATION RULES
============================================================

Existing visualizations must not be removed.

Improve them.

Prioritize:

SVG
Canvas
CSS transforms
GSAP
interactive sliders
drag interactions

Use SVG for mathematical diagrams where possible.

SVG diagrams should:
- scale responsively
- have clear labels
- animate drawing
- animate transformations
- highlight relationships

Avoid static screenshots for mathematical concepts.

============================================================
18. COLOR CODING MATHEMATICS
============================================================

Use consistent semantic colors.

Example:

angle:
coral

opposite side:
blue

adjacent side:
green

hypotenuse:
yellow

function graph:
lavender

Do not assign random colors to the same mathematical object on different pages.

Create reusable CSS variables.

Example:

:root {
  --bg: #F7F8F5;
  --surface: #FFFFFF;
  --text: #171A24;
  --muted: #6F737D;
  --border: rgba(23,26,36,.10);

  --coral: #FF695D;
  --blue: #7CA7D9;
  --green: #87B89A;
  --yellow: #F4C95D;
  --lavender: #A79BD8;
}

============================================================
19. CARDS
============================================================

Cards should not all look identical.

Use a mixture of:

- large feature cards
- compact topic cards
- visual cards
- horizontal cards
- editorial blocks
- interactive panels

Cards may have:

- subtle border
- tiny shadow
- hover translation
- image/visual layer
- animated accent

Avoid:

every section = 3-column card grid.

============================================================
20. HOVER INTERACTIONS
============================================================

Use subtle motion.

Good:

transform: translateY(-4px)

Good:

arrow moves 4px

Good:

border changes subtly

Good:

visual scales 1.02

Avoid:

large rotations

Avoid:

bouncing

Avoid:

aggressive scaling

Buttons should feel physical but restrained.

============================================================
21. MAGNETIC BUTTONS
============================================================

Magnetic buttons may be implemented with GSAP.

Use only on:
- major CTA
- navigation CTA
- important exploration CTA

Do not make every button magnetic.

Movement should be tiny:

approximately 5–12px.

============================================================
22. ROADMAP
============================================================

The roadmap is a core product feature.

Make it visually impressive.

Use:

- animated connection lines
- progressive node reveals
- active-node highlighting
- module grouping
- subtle path animation

Do not turn the roadmap into a generic flowchart.

Important:

The roadmap must show modules first.

For Trigonometry:

Introduction to Trigonometry
Trigonometric Ratios & Functions
Trigonometric Equations

Applications must NOT appear as a fourth module.

============================================================
23. DATA PRESERVATION
============================================================

DO NOT modify mathematical JSON content unless explicitly requested.

DO NOT rename concept IDs.

DO NOT rename exploration IDs.

DO NOT remove formulas.

DO NOT remove explanations.

DO NOT remove visualizations.

DO NOT modify backend API contracts.

UI should adapt around the existing data.

============================================================
24. COMPONENTIZATION
============================================================

Create reusable UI components where appropriate.

Potential components:

AnimatedSection
PageTransition
MagneticButton
RevealText
SectionHeading
AnimatedCard
InteractiveCard
RoadmapNode
AnimatedConnector
VisualizationFrame
ScrollProgress
NumberCounter

Do not create components for trivial one-line markup.

============================================================
25. CSS
============================================================

Prefer reusable CSS classes / CSS variables.

Avoid giant inline style objects.

Do not introduce Tailwind if the project does not already use it.

Follow the existing styling architecture.

Preserve responsive behavior.

Mobile is not an afterthought.

============================================================
26. RESPONSIVE DESIGN
============================================================

Desktop:
large editorial compositions

Tablet:
collapse complex compositions gracefully

Mobile:
single-column
large readable typography
touch-friendly controls

Interactive sliders must remain usable on mobile.

Do not rely on hover for essential functionality.

============================================================
27. ACCESSIBILITY
============================================================

Maintain:

- semantic HTML
- keyboard navigation
- visible focus states
- accessible labels
- sufficient contrast
- reduced-motion support

Interactive visualizations need accessible descriptions.

============================================================
28. PERFORMANCE
============================================================

Do not sacrifice performance for animation.

Avoid:

- huge libraries for trivial effects
- continuous animation loops
- unnecessary DOM nodes
- expensive blur filters
- huge canvas rendering
- repeated ScrollTrigger creation

Use transforms and opacity whenever possible.

Lazy load heavy visualization components if appropriate.

============================================================
29. ICONS
============================================================

The project already uses Lucide icons.

Continue using Lucide.

Do not introduce another icon library.

Icons should support meaning, not decoration.

============================================================
30. CONTENT HIERARCHY
============================================================

Do not make the UI so visually impressive that the content becomes difficult to read.

Priority:

1. Mathematical concept
2. Explanation
3. Visualization
4. Interaction
5. Decorative motion

Never reverse this order.

============================================================
31. AI/TUTOR UI
============================================================

The tutor should feel integrated into the learning environment.

Avoid making it look like a generic chatbot.

Use:

- contextual prompts
- concept-aware actions
- subtle panel animation
- clear explanation hierarchy

Do not modify AI backend behavior unless explicitly requested.

============================================================
32. WHEN MODIFYING A PAGE
============================================================

Before changing a page:

1. Read the entire component.
2. Inspect imported components.
3. Inspect relevant data structures.
4. Identify existing interactions.
5. Preserve them.
6. Improve visual hierarchy.
7. Add motion.
8. Test routing.
9. Test mobile layout.
10. Test TypeScript build.

============================================================
33. DO NOT DO THESE THINGS
============================================================

Never:

- create a dark background for the entire application
- rewrite the application from scratch
- delete existing content
- replace React Router
- replace the JSON content architecture
- introduce unnecessary dependencies
- create fake data
- hardcode mathematical content that already exists in JSON
- break Cloudflare Worker API routes
- break `/api/*`
- break direct route loading
- use Barba.js as a replacement router
- animate everything continuously
- add random decorative blobs everywhere
- make the site look like a generic AI SaaS product

============================================================
34. IMPLEMENTATION ORDER
============================================================

When asked to redesign the UI, implement in this order:

PHASE 1
Global design tokens
Typography
Backgrounds
Colors
Buttons
Cards

PHASE 2
Navbar
Page transitions
Global GSAP setup

PHASE 3
Landing page
Subjects
Roadmaps

PHASE 4
Trigonometry landing/module page

PHASE 5
Applications page

PHASE 6
Introduction to Trigonometry visualizations

PHASE 7
Topic/lesson pages

PHASE 8
Tutor experience

PHASE 9
Responsive/mobile polish

PHASE 10
Accessibility
Performance
Build verification

============================================================
35. BUILD REQUIREMENT
============================================================

After substantial changes run:

npm run build

The project must compile with:

TypeScript
Vite
React

Do not silence TypeScript errors merely to make the build pass.

Fix the underlying issue.

============================================================
36. FINAL DESIGN TEST
============================================================

Before considering the redesign complete, verify:

- Is the page light?
- Does the first viewport create curiosity?
- Is the hierarchy obvious?
- Does animation have a purpose?
- Can the student understand where to click?
- Does the UI feel mathematical?
- Does it feel premium?
- Does it work without animation?
- Does it work on mobile?
- Are existing concepts preserved?
- Are existing routes preserved?
- Are existing visualizations preserved?
- Does `npm run build` pass?

The result should feel like a polished educational product,
not a redesigned template.

============================================================
END OF CURSOR RULES
============================================================